\section{Scorecard scale}{


\code{base_score}, \code{base_odds} and \code{pdo} are one statement: \emph{at \code{base_score}
points the odds are \code{base_odds}, and every \code{pdo} points they double}.
\code{base_odds} is always expressed in the orientation \code{direction} implies
(non-event:event under \code{higher_is_safer}; event:non-event under
\code{higher_is_riskier}), and the alignment object records
\code{odds_orientation} so this is never implicit. The classic 600/50/20 is
Siddiqi's (2006) textbook example, not a parameter published by any
bureau. \code{align_method = "regression"} (default) takes the raw score to
that scale by regressing empirical log-odds on score bands, which absorbs
reweighting, miscalibration and prior shift; \code{"direct"} assumes the model
is calibrated and uses the logit as is.
